speechtext:
Oh, I am not particular;
I will say there has been ever since rural mail delivery was established, if that will give the minister any comfort. There is now, has been, and I will say always will be, as long as the present system is continued. It is desirable that that be removed. I think the minister will agree that in rural communities these miserable little contracts are a source of grievances and bitternesses that persist and are out of all proportion to the importance of the contract. So I suggest that for the purpose of doing away with this miserable, petty, contemptible patronage, the system that has been suggested should be adopted. Perhaps the selection should not be made by the department; I would be prepared to trust the inspector of the department or the minister, but it would be always under the suspicion that there was patronage in it. So perhaps the civil service commission is the only organization that is free from patronage or the suspicion of patronage in that direction. They could make the selection.
Once a man has a contract he must equip himself; he has to buy a motor car or some sort of vehicle, and he has his contract for only four years. Then up comes the patronage question again. The member of parliament is bothered; he is notified of the expiration of this contract and asked whether he recommends that it be renewed or that it should be put up for tender. He is the victim of pressure in his own community, and it would be better for him if he had nothing whatever to do with it.
